      "message": "[Medium] Matching any key that contains `password`/`secret` also redacts fields that are not secret. This happens both in `sanitize_data` and in the text fallback (`_SENSITIVE_TEXT_RE`):\n\n```\n{\"passwordless\": \"***\", \"i_password_policy\": \"***\", \"password_expired\": \"***\",\n \"is_password_set\": \"***\", \"password_lifetime\": \"***\"}\n```\n\n`password_expired` and `i_password_policy` are exactly what you look for when investigating why a sign-in fails, so hiding them costs real diagnostics and protects nothing.\n\nOptions:\n- redact only string values, and pass bools and numbers through as they are; or\n- narrow the rule to a whole word or suffix (`password`, `*_password`, `*_secret`).\n\nOn the positive side, `one_time_password` (the `AccessControl/verify_otp` param) is now hidden only because of this rule. Whatever variant is chosen, please pin that key in a test so it does not regress.",
